  "text": " Two sources tell NBC News that the Food and Drug Administration is expected to approve the new COVID-19 vaccine. The news comes as the U.S. is experiencing the largest summer surge of infections in two years. Manufacturers say they have the doses ready to go and are now just waiting for that final FDA approval. Tell us about these vaccines that we were mentioning in regards to getting this approval. What is the difference and what do people know about them? They're said to be substantially improved from what they were before. That's the Pfizer spokesperson who's talking about that. Moderna execs say millions of doses of its updated COVID vaccine are ready. We're anticipating the FDA determination of the updated COVID vaccine imminently, which would mean that the vaccine would be in the system within days thereafter. Moderna's VP of Medical Affairs in North America explains mRNA allows for COVID vaccines to be updated faster. We are only given the information after the FDA deliberated and their advisors made the recommendation on what should be the formulation, what variant should be in this updated vaccine, was in there six weeks ago. I think we're also going to start seeing people stop being quite so fearful of the vaccine. It's been around for several years now. It is tried and true. It is not causing the severe outcome in terms of side effects that a lot of people were afraid of. The CDC recommends everyone over six months old get an updated COVID and flu shot this year. Both updated vaccines will start to be available at pharmacies and in your doctor's offices. So make a plan now to get your updated vaccine in September or October. I'm going to get one myself. I believe in these vaccines and I want to make sure that my patients get them when they agree to do it. Let's start off with a check of your next weather meteorologist, Alyssa Carlson. She joins us live in the studio. Alyssa, this really is the calm before the storm. Not again! No! Oh!",
